            <ArticleTitle>Association of headache with childhood adversity and mental disorder: cross-national study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>111-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1192/bjp.bp.108.052159</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Community studies about the association of headache with both childhood family adversities and depression/anxiety disorders are limited.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the independent and joint associations of childhood family adversities and early-onset depression and anxiety disorders with risks of adult-onset headache.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data were pooled from cross-sectional community surveys conducted in ten Latin and North American, European and Asian countries (n=18 303) by using standardised instruments. Headache and a range of childhood family adversities were assessed by self-report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The number of childhood family adversities was associated with adult-onset headache after adjusting for gender, age, country and early-onset depression/anxiety disorder status (for one adversity, hazard ratio (HR)=1.22-1.6; for two adversities, HR=1.19-1.67; for three or more adversities, HR=1.37-1.95). Early and current onset of depression/anxiety disorders were independently associated (HR=1.42-1.89) with adult-onset headache after controlling for number of childhood family adversities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The findings call for a broad developmental perspective concerning risk factors for development of headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
